WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:01.720
Hello.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:06.360
I'm going to talk to you about the semi-conservative replication of DNA,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.180
not so much about the technical details,

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:14.310
but about how Frank Stahl and I ended up doing the experiment 

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.390
that showed that DNA replicates by the two chains coming apart,

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:23.420
each making a new copy and then you get two, each of which has one 

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.470
old chain and one new one.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.800
It's hard to know where to start any particular story,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.780
but let me start with the publication in 1953 of the paper by 

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.820
Watson and Crick.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:37.400
There were actually two papers in Nature.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.300
The first was about the structure,

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.300
which was based on model building and a little bit of information from X-ray 

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.250
diffraction.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.150
The X-ray diffraction certainly did not tell you the structure,

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:54.190
it told you something about the repeat distance along the helix, it told you that it 

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.550
was a helix, and very little more.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.000
The model building was model building,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.690
so the structure certainly wasn't proven.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.160
This was a proposal,

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:07.580
and many people didn't believe it, or maybe didn't even pay any attention to it.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.810
The second paper proposed how the molecule might replicate-- the two 

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.340
chains would separate,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.020
each would guide on its surface the formation of a new chain.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.040
And so you end up with two double helices,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:26.210
each one has one of the old chains and one brand new chain. That's called 

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.220
semi-conservative replication.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:29.950
At Caltech,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.520
Max Delbrück,

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.580
who knew Jim Watson, was in correspondence with him,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.440
was pessimistic about this replication scheme,

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.530
pessimistic about semi-conservative replication.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.940
The chains were wound around each other,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.380
so to get them apart,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.530
unless you break them,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.150
you'd have to unwind the basic double helix to get the two arms separate.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.590
Max thought this would be impossible,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.350
hydrodynamically impossible.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.710
And so he proposed that maybe the two chains separate by a system of breaking 

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.360
every several nucleotides along the chain.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.830
And he and Gunther Stent published a paper which proposed three different methods 

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.280
for DNA replication:

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.700
semi-conservative,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.390
as Watson and Crick had predicted,

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.180
conservative in which, at least conceptually,

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.950
maybe there was a way that a double helix could guide the formation of another double helix 

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.750
just like it nearby and so you never have to separate the 

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:27.630
chains at all,

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.560
you'd have brand new double helix and a completely old double helix,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.250
and that would be the active replication, and then dispersive,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.190
the third way, break that single chains separate the pieces 

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.130
and then put everything back together again.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.130
I visited Max in his office.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.300
I was in chemistry.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.280
I was a student of Linus Pauling.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.250
Max was over in biology and he told me about this problem.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.650
And it occurred to me that maybe one could do an experiment to find out the 

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.790
mode of replication of DNA based on the use of heavy isotopes.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.510
I wish I had time to tell you about why I thought about heavy isotopes,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.220
but it did have to do with taking a course,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.890
which was a piece of luck,

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:15.730
Linus Pauling's course on the nature of the chemical bond, in which deuterium and 

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.070
hydrogen bonds played an important role.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.360
Nevertheless,

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.120
the idea was to label DNA with something heavy,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.770
I don't think I thought about--

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:25.110
yes,

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.160
it was deuterium I thought about at the time,

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:31.450
and then to label it by growing bacteria,

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.240
or phages, in heavy medium, deuterium medium,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.720
and then switch the growth to light medium,

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:41.920
and then put all of this in the centrifuge and look to see where the DNA went. Up 

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.330
to the top-- if you adjusted the density right--

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:49.570
if it was all light, down to the bottom if it was all heavy, and in the middle if 

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.330
it was half heavy and half light.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.350
That's an oversimplification,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.760
but that's the way I thought about the experiment at that time.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.920
This was around 1954 sometime. I then went to Woods 

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.170
Hole as a teaching assistant for Jim Watson,

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.650
he had lived at Caltech the previous term,

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.250
in the physiology course.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.920
And one day as a teaching assistant in that course,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.080
Jim Watson and Sydney Brenner,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.300
who were teaching the course,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.520
were in an upstairs room in the building called the Lillie Building at 

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.280
the Marine Biological Lab,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:30.050
and Jim looked through the window and pointed down at a tree under which was sitting a man.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.210
He had a big jug of gin and a big thing of tonic and some ice and glasses;

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.230
it was called the gin and tonic tree.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.020
But Jim was saying,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:40.310
this guy thought pretty much of himself,

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.280
so let's give him a really tough experiment to do in the physiology class-- the famous 

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.970
Hershey-Chase experiment, done by two people over a period of time,

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.580
Hershey and Chase. See if Stahl could do it all in one afternoon by himself.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:53.290
Well,

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:57.980
I thought that was pretty rotten to gang up on this poor guy so I went down and introduced 

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.230
myself to him and told him what lay in store for him.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.380
And he told me what he was doing in phage genetics,

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.900
and he would be at Caltech that next year.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:12.010
So we agreed to try to do this experiment together, ourselves, on how DNA replicates 

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.510
when he got to Caltech.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.990
I had to finish my X-ray crystallography first before Frank would let us start because 

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:23.690
he said it would be bad for my character to go ahead and start some new project when I 

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.880
hadn't finished my my thesis work,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.550
which was X-ray crystallography.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:28.490
Finally,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.560
I got the X-ray crystallography done,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.450
and we could start the experiment.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:38.100
Frank and I decided that we should develop the method for doing this density gradient centrifugation,

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:44.940
and so we spent quite a long time, more than a year, developing a method that could separate 

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:49.690
macromolecules in a density gradient. And to do that we put in pure DNA 

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:50.950
into a centrifuge,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.700
we turned it on to see what was happening,

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:55.220
and to our amazement,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.330
a density gradient was forming before our very eyes.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.533
This was because the cesium ion is quite heavy,

00:06:03.533 --> 00:06:04.453
quite dense,

00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:09.573
and it tends to settle to the bottom in a powerful centrifugal field and 

00:06:09.583 --> 00:06:13.813
diffusion wants it to go back up to redistribute it. And at equilibrium,

00:06:13.813 --> 00:06:18.743
you have a density gradient in which there's more cesium chloride near the bottom than 

00:06:18.743 --> 00:06:19.433
near the top.

00:06:20.063 --> 00:06:22.823
And so the density gradient forms automatically.

00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:25.383
We didn't expect this when we saw it happen.

00:06:25.383 --> 00:06:29.863
We thought we'd have to make a preformed density gradient in the centrifuge 

00:06:30.613 --> 00:06:31.133
cell.

00:06:31.723 --> 00:06:34.463
But the centrifuge itself makes the density gradient.

00:06:35.063 --> 00:06:36.783
So to make a long story short,

00:06:37.013 --> 00:06:40.083
we grew bacteria in heavy nitrogen medium,

00:06:40.363 --> 00:06:44.913
and then after many generations of growth so that everything would be 

00:06:45.053 --> 00:06:46.603
labeled with heavy nitrogen,

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:51.513
we switched the bacteria centrifuging them and resuspending them in light 

00:06:51.513 --> 00:06:54.803
medium and took samples at various times.

00:06:54.993 --> 00:06:59.983
The first experiment, Frank had warned me I would mix it up if I did both 

00:06:59.983 --> 00:07:00.983
directions at once,

00:07:00.983 --> 00:07:03.333
that is heavy to light and light to heavy.

00:07:03.603 --> 00:07:04.373
And I said,

00:07:04.373 --> 00:07:04.643
no,

00:07:04.643 --> 00:07:06.423
I'll color code the tubes.

00:07:06.623 --> 00:07:08.533
I mixed it up completely,

00:07:08.783 --> 00:07:12.633
so it was unclear exactly which tubes were which.

00:07:12.823 --> 00:07:15.843
The second experiment we labeled Experiment Number 1,

00:07:16.263 --> 00:07:17.603
and that's what we published,

00:07:17.713 --> 00:07:19.063
along with Experiment Number 2,

00:07:19.313 --> 00:07:20.673
which was a repeat.

00:07:20.803 --> 00:07:21.793
And what we found,

00:07:21.853 --> 00:07:22.493
of course,

00:07:22.623 --> 00:07:27.613
was that the bacteria at one generation 

00:07:27.743 --> 00:07:29.453
had only one kind of DNA.

00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:32.343
It had a density halfway between heavy and light.

00:07:32.763 --> 00:07:34.983
And in the second bacterial generation,

00:07:35.203 --> 00:07:38.733
there were two kinds of DNA, half heavy and fully light.

00:07:39.123 --> 00:07:43.093
What we did was blessed by a lot of accidents.

00:07:43.273 --> 00:07:45.003
The accident of being at Caltech,

00:07:45.163 --> 00:07:47.213
the accident of meeting each other at Woods Hole,

00:07:47.523 --> 00:07:52.113
the accident of having Max Delbrück impress you with his deep pessimism 

00:07:52.383 --> 00:07:57.233
that DNA couldn't possibly replicate the Watson and Crick way, the way it does,

00:07:57.623 --> 00:07:58.393
and finally,

00:07:58.403 --> 00:08:02.932
the accident of finding out that the centrifuge itself will make a density gradient,

00:08:03.192 --> 00:08:05.582
you don't have to make a preexisting one.

00:08:06.242 --> 00:08:07.952
Now we had to write this paper up.

00:08:08.492 --> 00:08:08.952
Actually,

00:08:08.952 --> 00:08:13.832
we were rather sluggish in writing it up and so Max Delbrück took us to the marine lab of 

00:08:13.832 --> 00:08:18.552
Caltech at Corona Del Mar and locked us up in a tower room, literally did. 

00:08:19.042 --> 00:08:19.532
Mary Delbrück,

00:08:19.812 --> 00:08:24.432
Max's wife, would bring us meals, but then lock the door again 

00:08:24.652 --> 00:08:28.812
until we-- and there was a typewriter-- until we produced a draft manuscript,

00:08:28.822 --> 00:08:29.502
which we did.

00:08:29.992 --> 00:08:34.702
The effect of this experiment is worth saying something about. When the DNA structure was 

00:08:34.702 --> 00:08:35.532
proposed,

00:08:35.542 --> 00:08:37.182
a lot of people didn't believe it,

00:08:37.302 --> 00:08:42.122
and there were not a lot of references to it in the literature for the first several years after 

00:08:42.122 --> 00:08:43.452
1953.

00:08:43.842 --> 00:08:44.452
After all,

00:08:44.452 --> 00:08:46.022
it was based on model building,

00:08:46.022 --> 00:08:47.392
which doesn't prove anything.

00:08:47.972 --> 00:08:52.932
It looked so beautiful that some people were convinced that it had to be right because 

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:53.952
it looked so right.

00:08:54.642 --> 00:08:55.612
Other people,

00:08:55.612 --> 00:08:59.502
I think, were convinced it couldn't be right because it looked too good to be true.

00:09:00.302 --> 00:09:01.262
In any case,

00:09:01.582 --> 00:09:03.292
it was just a model.

00:09:03.522 --> 00:09:08.042
The evidence from the X-ray diffraction was really not very supportive.

00:09:08.042 --> 00:09:12.562
It showed that repeat distance was a certain distance and that it was helical,

00:09:13.072 --> 00:09:17.252
but you couldn't deduce any of the details from those early X-ray pictures.

00:09:18.092 --> 00:09:21.852
I think the effect of our experiment wasn't really a discovery,

00:09:22.242 --> 00:09:23.802
it was a psychological effect.

00:09:23.802 --> 00:09:28.322
It made the DNA seem real. Suddenly you could see bands in a 

00:09:28.322 --> 00:09:32.652
centrifuge that were behaving just the way Watson and Crick said they should.

00:09:33.042 --> 00:09:37.722
And I think that the main value of the experiment was it had the 

00:09:37.722 --> 00:09:42.452
psychological value of convincing a lot of people that the DNA 

00:09:42.452 --> 00:09:44.102
structure had to be right.

00:09:44.902 --> 00:09:46.812
Let me say something about that molecule.

00:09:47.282 --> 00:09:51.852
This molecule essentially gave orders to a whole period in the 

00:09:51.852 --> 00:09:53.532
development of molecular biology.

00:09:53.762 --> 00:09:55.902
Here's this double helix standing there,

00:09:56.082 --> 00:09:56.682
and it's saying,

00:09:56.692 --> 00:09:57.632
"Here I am,

00:09:57.802 --> 00:09:58.742
I have two chains,

00:09:58.742 --> 00:10:00.352
go find out how I replicate.

00:10:01.042 --> 00:10:01.742
Here I am,

00:10:01.742 --> 00:10:03.562
I have four kinds of bases,

00:10:03.841 --> 00:10:07.191
go figure out how that is converted into making proteins.

00:10:07.681 --> 00:10:09.051
I sit in the nucleus,

00:10:09.051 --> 00:10:13.931
proteins are made out of the cytoplasm in eukaryotes, go figure out what it is that takes this 

00:10:13.931 --> 00:10:18.801
information from me out to the cytoplasm. My information being made up of these 

00:10:18.801 --> 00:10:20.521
bases changes once in a while,

00:10:20.521 --> 00:10:21.781
that's called mutation,

00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:23.801
go figure out how that happens.

00:10:24.181 --> 00:10:25.361
Every once in a while,

00:10:25.511 --> 00:10:27.821
there's something called genetic recombination,

00:10:27.951 --> 00:10:32.481
go figure out how I'm..." What I'm trying to say here is that if I showed you a model of, say,

00:10:32.481 --> 00:10:32.961
a polysaccharide,

00:10:34.001 --> 00:10:36.421
would it tell you what experiment you should do next?

00:10:36.971 --> 00:10:40.981
This molecule DNA is like the Wizard of Oz, standing there,

00:10:40.991 --> 00:10:45.841
except unlike the Wizard this is a true molecule, standing there and telling 

00:10:45.841 --> 00:10:50.821
you essentially the whole agenda for the future of science for the 

00:10:50.821 --> 00:10:51.991
next 20 years.

00:10:52.511 --> 00:10:56.051
That's a completely different mood and attitude,

00:10:56.061 --> 00:11:00.971
I think, from the science that went before it and the biological science that came after 

00:11:00.971 --> 00:11:01.121
it.

00:11:01.611 --> 00:11:06.531
So that was the characteristic of this time. It was the DNA molecule telling you what the 

00:11:06.531 --> 00:11:07.371
problems were,

00:11:07.541 --> 00:11:09.321
what you had to go out and solve.

